    {
      "id": "MJUe",
      "code_hash": "041bead1544311c2df90369ad2ce71a0",
      "outputs": [
        {
          "type": "data",
          "data": {
            "text/markdown": "<span class=\"markdown prose dark:prose-invert contents\"><h1 id=\"rolling-downhill\">\u26f0\ufe0f Rolling downhill</h1>\n<h3 id=\"structure-optimization-with-ase-and-the-uma-machine-learning-potential\">Structure optimization with ASE and the UMA machine-learning potential</h3>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">Every arrangement of atoms has an <strong>energy</strong>. Nature prefers low energy, so molecules and crystals\nsettle into shapes where nudging any atom in any direction would only raise the energy.\nFinding that shape on a computer is called <strong>structure optimization</strong> (or <em>geometry relaxation</em>),\nand it is the first step of almost every computational chemistry and materials science workflow.</span>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">The idea fits in one sentence: <strong>follow the forces until they vanish.</strong> The force on each atom\npoints downhill on the energy landscape, so we move the atoms along the forces, recompute,\nand repeat until every force is tiny.</span>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">In this notebook you will:</span>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Be the optimizer</strong> by stepping a two-atom molecule downhill by hand.</li>\n<li><strong>Relax a real molecule</strong> that we deliberately scramble, and watch it find its way home.</li>\n<li><strong>Race the optimizers</strong> that ship with ASE against each other.</li>\n<li><strong>Relax a crystal</strong>, including the size of its unit cell, and read off its lattice constant and stiffness.</li>\n</ol>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">Everything is live: change a control and the cells that depend on it re-run automatically.</span>\n<div class=\"admonition\">\n<span class=\"admonition-title\">\u25b6\ufe0f Only seeing text, no sliders or plots?</span>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">The notebook hasn't been run yet. Switch to the editor view and click <strong>Run all</strong> (or set\n<em>Settings \u2192 Runtime \u2192 On startup</em> to <strong>autorun</strong>), then switch back to app view.\nThe first run installs the packages and downloads the UMA model, so give it a minute or two.\nYou also need a (free) Hugging Face token with access to UMA: see section 0.</span>\n</div>\n<details>\n<summary>\ud83e\uddf0 The tools we're using</summary>\n<ul>\n<li><strong><a href=\"https://wiki.fysik.dtu.dk/ase/\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">ASE</a></strong> (the Atomic Simulation Environment) is a Python library for\n  building, manipulating, and simulating atomic structures. It provides the <code>Atoms</code> object,\n  the optimizers, and a common <em>calculator</em> interface for computing energies and forces.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"https://huggingface.co/facebook/UMA\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">UMA</a></strong> (Universal Model for Atoms, from Meta's\n  <a href=\"https://github.com/facebookresearch/fairchem\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">FAIR Chemistry</a> team) is a universal machine-learning\n  interatomic potential. It was trained on hundreds of millions of quantum-mechanical (DFT) calculations\n  spanning molecules, materials, catalysts, and more, and it reproduces DFT energies and forces across most\n  of the periodic table while running thousands of times faster. That speed is what makes this notebook\n  interactive. We use the small model, <strong>UMA-S 1.2.1</strong> (<code>uma-s-1p2p1</code>).</li>\n</ul>\n</details></span>"
          }
        }
      ],
      "console": []
    },
