    {
      "id": "aLJB",
      "code_hash": "160812ac71e4967b6d82604ca0ec56db",
      "outputs": [
        {
          "type": "data",
          "data": {
            "text/markdown": "<span class=\"markdown prose dark:prose-invert contents\"><hr />\n<h2 id=\"3-the-optimizer-race\">3 \u00b7 The optimizer race \ud83c\udfc1</h2>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">Steepest descent is simple but slow. ASE ships smarter algorithms that differ in how they decide\n<strong>where to step next</strong>:</span>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Optimizer</th>\n<th>Idea in one line</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>BFGS</strong></td>\n<td>Builds up an estimate of the landscape's curvature (the Hessian) and jumps toward the bottom of the local bowl. The usual default.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>LBFGS</strong></td>\n<td>Same idea, but remembers only the last few steps. Uses far less memory for big systems.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>FIRE</strong></td>\n<td>Rolls like a ball with friction (molecular dynamics), stopping whenever it starts going uphill. Very robust.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>MDMin</strong></td>\n<td>Also dynamics-based: moves atoms like a ball and kills the velocity when it points uphill.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>GPMin</strong></td>\n<td>Fits a Gaussian-process surrogate to the energies seen so far and minimizes that. Few steps, but each one costs more.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">All five start from <strong>the same shaken molecule you set up above</strong>, so change the molecule or\nthe shake and the race re-runs.</span></span>"
          }
        }
      ],
      "console": []
    },
